    "body": "<!-- pr-reviewer: ae628c2f984cc125b7c9dd46570f8051309ab5c5 -->\n**Review (M5): needs changes before merge. The fix direction is right, but the pins are too loose for a test that guards a gouge verdict.**\n\n**Measured on macOS (Apple silicon), release, at this head:** `moves 6503 planar 6467 arcs 222`. Rings fallbacks at exactly **Z 20.000 and Z 10.000**, both \"crosses itself\". The test passes in 107.5 s. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -D warnings` is clean. The trial merge with upstream/main was already up to date.\n\nSo this Mac differs from main's pin (6500/6464/222) by **3 moves and 3 planar moves only**. Arcs (222) and the fallback set are identical across the platforms.\n\n**What's kept (good):** `warns.is_empty()` (it verifies clean), no planning-budget or too-detailed refusal, each rings level names its Z with \"Offset rings\", and `arcs > 0 || !fell.is_empty()`.\n\n**Requested changes (AGENTS.md: coverage must not ride away):**\n1. **Move band:** `5000..=8000` is about ±23% around 6500, against a measured 3-move platform spread. A planner regression of hundreds of moves would pass. Use a band sized to the spread, e.g. `6450..=6560` (or ±1%) for moves, with planar to match.\n2. **Arcs:** `arcs > 0` replaced an exact 222, which is identical on both platforms. Keep `arcs == 222`, or at most a narrow band around it.\n3. **Fallback set:** \"includes Z 20.000, at most 2\" replaced an exact set that is identical on both platforms. Pin the set exactly as `{Z 20.000, Z 10.000}`, so a level that stops falling back, or a new one that starts, fails the test.\n\n#1010 is right to track the float difference. It could add the post-#689 pair (macOS 6503/6467/222 vs main 6500/6464/222), which the PR body already carries.\n\nTurned back to draft per the review rules; flag it ready again after the push for a delta review.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
